From d04953f757f5aee7da18841d6ae293f1655d4b63 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oh My Pi Harness <omp@example.invalid>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 01:24:13 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] chassishandler: clear the one-time boot flag when the
 override is cleared

The BIOS consumes a one-time boot override by sending Set System Boot
Options parameter 5 with the boot flag valid bit cleared, no boot device
and the permanent bit clear.  The handler mapped 'permanent clear' to
the one-time object unconditionally, so the clear re-armed the one-time
flag instead of dropping it.  bmcweb then kept reporting/storing a
one-time override that no longer existed.

Only set the one-time flag when the request is a valid override; a clear
drops it.

Upstream-Status: Pending

Agent/generator: Oh My Pi harness, model opencode-go/deepseek-v4.1-flash.
---
 chassishandler.cpp | 5 ++++-
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/chassishandler.cpp b/chassishandler.cpp
index b6a7532..359bc42 100644
--- a/chassishandler.cpp
+++ b/chassishandler.cpp
@@ -2260,7 +2260,10 @@ ipmi::RspType<> ipmiChassisSetSysBootOptions(ipmi::Context::ptr ctx,
 
         try
         {
-            rc = setBootOneTime(ctx, !permanent);
+            // A request with the boot flag valid bit cleared means "no
+            // boot override"; this is how the BIOS consumes a one-time
+            // override.  Do not turn a clear into a new one-time request.
+            rc = setBootOneTime(ctx, validFlag && !permanent);
             if (rc != ipmi::ccSuccess)
             {
                 return ipmi::response(rc);
